WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.400
Narrator: Saturday, 12
p.m., the light burns,

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.670
your head throbs, and
you have no recollection

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.810
of how you got back home.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.290
Don't worry, you're not alone.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:15.320
More than half of college
students experience blackouts,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.430
according to several studies.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.810
And let's be clear, blacking
out doesn't mean passing out.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.310
You were probably awake
and aware the entire night.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.163
So then, where did all those memories go?

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.840
Let's rewind to Friday night.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.510
Normally, whenever you have an experience,

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.910
like a conversation, a part of your brain

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.990
called the prefrontal cortex
stores that information

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.010
in short-term memory.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:45.400
Then, another part of your
brain called the hippocampus

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.400
weaves those experiences together,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.640
so they can be stored away
as long-term memories.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.480
So the next day, you remember
the party as a whole,

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.450
instead of smell of sweat,
house music, Jen was there.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.090
But here's the key part.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.630
Storing these episodes in long-term memory

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.470
requires special neurotransmitters.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.730
But your liquor shots
prevent the neurotransmitters

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.200
from working properly.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.050
So instead of remembering the party,

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.950
all you have is an incomplete,
or even empty file.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.490
And the amount of alcohol
in your system at the time

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.180
influences how much you remember.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.490
Let's say you're a 73 kilogram adult man,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.300
and you've done eight shots in one hour.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.410
Your blood alcohol content is probably

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.880
around .2% by this point,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.230
more than twice the legal
limit for driving a car.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.710
And your brain may still be
able to store some memories,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.890
so you end up with islands of memories

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.400
separated by missing sections.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.840
That's called a fragmentary blackout,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.240
aka a grayout or brownout.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.450
But if you keep pounding
those shots, it gets worse.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.140
Within the next half hour,

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.510
you pound back another four shots.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.290
Now your blood alcohol
content hits around .3%,

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:04.290
and your hippocampus goes
dark, and full amnesia sets in.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.000
This is called an en bloc blackout,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.140
and once you wake up, that
entire night could be blank.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.610
Push your BAC much higher
than that, and you might die.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.220
And yet your friends
might not even realize

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.090
you're in the middle of a blackout,

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.260
since the alcohol didn't
delete your long-term memories

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.440
already safe in storage
before the night began.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.450
So you can still carry on conversations

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.560
and behave more or less like a
typical person, to an extent.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.160
Blackouts aside, alcohol
can still interfere

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.120
with other regions of your brain,

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.580
including those responsible

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.900
for reasoning and decision-making.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.880
So during blackouts,
people have crashed cars,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.000
gotten into fights, and committed

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.680
or been the victims of sexual assault.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.720
They might just not remember it.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.000
That being said, not
everyone gets blackouts.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.970
Your sex, body weight and
family history all play a role.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.100
So that could explain

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.120
why your friends recall the entire night,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.050
despite downing just as much tequila.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.670
But it won't save them

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.683
from a wicked hangover the next morning.

